A  Singular spectrum of the stacked regressor: the offset column dominates
==============================================================================
  sigma, parameter columns only: [3.044e-03 2.061e-03 1.060e-03 7.855e-04 6.264e-04 2.359e-04 1.935e-04 9.765e-05 4.366e-05 2.768e-05 3.359e-19 2.670e-19 1.989e-20 9.713e-21]
  sigma, with the offset column: [3.141e+00 3.043e-03 2.054e-03 1.060e-03 7.854e-04 6.256e-04 2.306e-04 1.932e-04 8.084e-05 3.414e-05 2.562e-05 1.273e-17 1.446e-18 1.188e-20 7.089e-21]
  sigma_max(with offset) / sigma_max(parameters only) = 1.032e+03
  The offset column is the nominal one-step transition f(theta_bar), whose
  position rows are q + Ts*qdot, order 0.3 m. Every parameter column is a
  derivative of that map, order 1e-3 or less. So the affine protected set is
  numerically dominated by ONE direction that is not a parameter direction.
  Consequence: a pinv cutoff stated relative to sigma_0 is a cutoff relative to
  the offset column, and at rcond >= 1e-4 it would silently discard REAL
  parameter directions.
    rcond 1e-02: rank(with offset) =  1   rank(parameters only) =  9
    rcond 1e-04: rank(with offset) =  6   rank(parameters only) = 10
    rcond 1e-06: rank(with offset) = 11   rank(parameters only) = 10
    rcond 1e-08: rank(with offset) = 11   rank(parameters only) = 10
    rcond 1e-10: rank(with offset) = 11   rank(parameters only) = 10
    rcond 1e-12: rank(with offset) = 11   rank(parameters only) = 10

